.Dd September 28, 2026
.Dt ROBBIE 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm robbie
.Nd make what is out of date, and show what can be done
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl iknpq
.Op Fl C Ar dir
.Op Fl f Ar file
.Op Fl j Ar n
.Op Ar name Ns = Ns Ar value ...
.Op Ar target ...
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
reads a robfile, which says what each file is made from and how, and
makes the named
.Ar targets ,
running only the recipes whose files are out of date.
With no
.Ar target ,
it lists everything the robfile can make.
.Pp
Each target is made after everything it needs, one at a time unless
.Fl j
allows more.
A recipe has its placeholders filled in, is echoed to standard output,
and is run as one script with
.Ql /bin/sh -e -c ,
so the first command in it that fails stops it.
It inherits the standard input, output and error of
.Nm ,
and its environment.
Before anything runs, every recipe in the robfile is checked for
unknown placeholders, so a typo stops
.Nm
at once rather than halfway through a build.
When a recipe fails,
.Nm
reports it and stops, unless
.Fl k
is given.
Recipes already running are waited for either way.
When a recipe does not succeed, and it has changed its target file,
the file is removed, so that the next run makes it again rather than
taking a half-made file for up to date.
A directory, and a file the recipe never touched, are left alone.
.Pp
On
.Dv SIGINT
or
.Dv SIGTERM ,
.Nm
passes the signal on to every recipe running, starts no more, waits
for them, removes the files they had begun to make, and then dies of
the same signal.
A signal that was ignored when
.Nm
started stays ignored by
.Nm
itself; each recipe starts with every signal as it is at first.
.Pp
Each target made starts with a header, its name after a small
triangle, above its recipe; a target with no recipe has none.
The run ends with a line saying how it went:
.Pp
.Dl done: 3 made, 1 up to date, in 0.42s
.Pp
or, when a recipe failed or a target was not made,
.Pp
.Dl failed: 1 made, 1 failed, 2 not made, in 0.05s
.Pp
counting the targets made, those whose recipe failed, those not made
because something they need was not, and the files already up to date,
leaving out each count that is 0.
On a terminal the headers are coloured, echoed recipes are dim, and
failures are red.
.Pp
The options are as follows:
.Bl -tag -width "-f file"
.It Fl C Ar dir
Change to
.Ar dir
before doing anything else.
.It Fl f Ar file
Read
.Ar file ,
relative to
.Ar dir
when
.Fl C
is given, instead of
.Pa Robfile .
.It Fl h , Fl \-help
Print a usage message and exit.
.It Fl i
Interactive: pick a target from the listing and make it, as described
under
.Sx Interactive mode .
No
.Ar target
may be named with it, and standard input and output must be a
terminal.
.It Fl j Ar n
Run up to
.Ar n
recipes at once, from 1 to 1000.
The default is 1.
A target starts once everything it needs is made; of those ready, the
one that would be made first one at a time starts first.
The output of recipes running together can interleave.
.It Fl k
Keep going: after a recipe fails, or when an input is missing and no
rule makes it, make whatever does not depend on it.
Each target that is not made because of it is reported.
.It Fl n
Print the recipes of the targets that are out of date, in the order
they would run, with their placeholders filled in, without running
them.
Each has its header, and there is no summary.
An input that would be made counts as newer than its target, for
.Cm {newer} .
.It Fl p
Print what
.Nm
read, as a robfile, and make nothing: first every variable with its
final value, then each rule with its description above it and its
recipe indented by four spaces.
Placeholders are filled in everywhere but in recipes, which are shown as
written, less the indentation of their first line.
Lines joined by a backslash are shown joined, except in recipes, which
keep them for the shell.
The robfile is not checked for loops.
.It Fl q
Quiet: do not echo recipe lines.
The headers and the summary are printed all the same.
With
.Fl n
they are printed all the same.
.El
.Pp
Flags can be bundled, as in
.Fl kn ,
and a value can follow its flag directly, as in
.Fl j Ns 4 .
The first word that is not a flag ends them, and so does
.Fl - .
.Pp
An operand of the form
.Ar name Ns = Ns Ar value
sets the variable
.Ar name
to
.Ar value ,
blanks and braces included: placeholders in it are not filled in.
The robfile cannot change a variable set this way.
It also goes into the environment of every recipe, as
.Ev name ,
so
.Ql robbie install DESTDIR=pkg
and
.Ql DESTDIR=pkg robbie install
both reach a recipe that reads
.Ev $DESTDIR .
A
.Ar name
is a letter or underscore followed by letters, digits and underscores.
It cannot be one of the placeholders
.Nm
fills in itself:
.Cm target ,
.Cm input ,
.Cm inputs ,
.Cm newer
or
.Cm stem .
Every other operand is a
.Ar target
to make, which some rule in the robfile must make: naming a file that
is only ever an input, or no file at all, is an error.
.Ss The listing
With no
.Ar target ,
.Nm
prints the path of the robfile, with the home directory written as
.Ql ~ ,
and then what it holds, in three parts:
.Bl -tag -width variables
.It tasks
Each task, with its description and, after an arrow, its inputs as
written.
.It files
Each file a rule makes, and each pattern, likewise.
A
.Ql \(bu
marks a file that is out of date; on a terminal it is yellow.
An input that is missing and that no rule makes is not an error here;
it only makes what needs it out of date.
.It variables
Each variable with its final value, in as many columns as fit.
.El
.Pp
The rules are in the order of the robfile, and their columns line up.
Text too long for the line is cut, ending in an ellipsis: the inputs
first, then the descriptions.
The line is as wide as the terminal, or 80 characters when standard
output is not a terminal.
.Pp
On a terminal the output is in colour: names are bold, tasks and files
each have their own colour, and inputs are dim.
There is no colour when standard output, or standard error for
messages, is not a terminal, or when
.Ev NO_COLOR
is set, so a script sees plain text.
.Ss Interactive mode
With
.Fl i ,
.Nm
shows the tasks and files of the listing, without the patterns and the
variables, on the terminal's alternate screen, with a cursor on one of
them.
The list scrolls when it is longer than the screen, and is drawn again
when the terminal changes size.
The keys are:
.Bl -tag -width "Up, Down"
.It Up , Down
Move the cursor; so do
.Cm j
and
.Cm k .
.It Enter
Make the target under the cursor, as if it had been named on the
command line, on the screen
.Nm
was started from.
With
.Fl n ,
only show what would run.
.It Cm n
Show what making the target would run, without running it.
.It Cm q
Quit.
.It Esc
Clear the filter, or quit when there is none.
.It Ctrl-C
Quit at once.
.El
.Pp
Any other letter, digit or sign is typed into a filter, and the list
shows only the names that hold what was typed, ignoring case.
While a filter is typed,
.Cm j ,
.Cm k ,
.Cm n
and
.Cm q
are typed into it like any other letter; Backspace takes one back.
.Pp
After a target is made,
.Nm
waits for a key and goes back to the list, where the marks of the files
out of date are updated and the summary of the run is shown at the
bottom.
An input that is missing, and that no rule makes, is reported as with
.Fl k ,
and does not end the interactive mode.
The terminal is put back as it was however
.Nm
ends; on
.Dv SIGINT ,
.Dv SIGTERM
or
.Dv SIGHUP
it is put back first, and
.Nm
then dies of the signal.
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
Recipes run with the environment
.Nm
was given, and each
.Ar name Ns = Ns Ar value
operand added to it.
.Bl -tag -width NO_COLOR
.It Ev HOME
The home directory, written as
.Ql ~
in the listing.
.It Ev NO_COLOR
When set, and not empty, there is no colour at all.
It is read from the environment
.Nm
was started with, so a
.Li NO_COLOR=1
operand reaches only the recipes.
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width Robfile
.It Pa Robfile
The robfile read when
.Fl f
is not given; its format is described in
.Xr robfile 5 .
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Nm
exits 0 on success, 1 when a recipe failed, and 2 when the robfile has
an error or
.Nm
is used wrongly.
Stopped by a signal, it dies of that signal, which a shell reports as
128 plus its number.
An input that is missing, and that no rule makes, counts as an error
in the robfile, even when
.Fl k
made everything else.
.Sh EXAMPLES
In robbie's own repository, whose robfile is the first example in
.Xr robfile 5 ,
.Nm
with no target shows what can be done there; on a terminal 80
characters wide, once robbie is built:
.Bd -literal
 robbie  ~/r/robbie/Robfile

 tasks
   test           Run the tests, then check the man pages.  \(<- build/robbie
   install        Install into /usr/local.                  \(<- build/robbie
   uninstall      Remove what install put there.
   clean          Remove everything the build made.

 files
   build/robbie   The program itself.                       \(<- src/*.raz
 \(bu robbie.tar.gz  A release tarball.                        \(<- build/robbie doc/\[u2026]

 variables
   raz = raz -Werror                     prefix = /usr/local
   pages = doc/robbie.1 doc/robfile.5
.Ed
.Pp
The tarball is out of date, since it has not been made yet.
Make it, with up to four recipes at once:
.Pp
.Dl $ robbie -j4 robbie.tar.gz
.Pp
See what installing would run, without running it:
.Pp
.Dl $ robbie -n install
.Pp
Install into a staging directory, under
.Pa /usr :
.Pp
.Dl $ robbie install prefix=/usr DESTDIR=pkg
.Pp
Pick a task from a list, and run it:
.Pp
.Dl $ robbie -i
.Sh DIAGNOSTICS
A mistake in the robfile is reported with the file and the line it
starts on, as in
.Pp
.Dl robbie: Robfile:12: unknown placeholder {rza}; did you mean {raz}?
.Pp
and
.Nm
stops there.
An unknown placeholder is reported with the closest name
.Nm
knows, when one is close enough to be a likely typo, and so is a
.Ar target
that no rule makes:
.Pp
.Dl robbie: no rule makes 'tset'; did you mean 'test'?
.Pp
A loop, where a target needs itself through other rules, is reported
whole, a line to each link, with the place of the rule that makes it:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
robbie: Robfile:3: 'main.o' needs itself, through a loop:
robbie: Robfile:3:   main.o needs gen.h
robbie: Robfile:5:   gen.h needs gen.sh
robbie: Robfile:6:   gen.sh needs main.o
.Ed
.Pp
An input that does not exist, and that no rule makes, is reported at
the rule that needs it, but only when a
.Ar target
asked for needs it:
.Pp
.Dl robbie: Robfile:5: 'b.o' needs 'b.c', which does not exist, and no rule makes it
.Pp
A glob that matches no file is reported wherever it is in the
robfile:
.Pp
.Dl robbie: Robfile:3: 'prog' needs '*.cc', which matches no file
.Pp
A recipe that fails is reported at the first line of its rule, with
its exit status or the signal that killed it:
.Pp
.Dl robbie: Robfile:2: 'prog' failed: exit status 1
.Pp
When that recipe, or one stopped by a signal, had begun to write its
target, the half-made file is removed and reported:
.Pp
.Dl robbie: Robfile:2: removed half-made 'prog'
.Pp
With
.Fl k ,
a target that is not made because something it needs was not is
reported too:
.Pp
.Dl robbie: Robfile:8: not making 'prog', since it needs 'b.o'
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr robfile 5 ,
.Xr make 1 ,
.Xr sh 1
